56
00:05:09,936 --> 00:05:11,216
So, what does it offer?

57
00:05:11,376 --> 00:05:12,816
What features does it offer?

58
00:05:13,006 --> 00:05:20,336
Well, it offers full document SSR, so server side rendering, streaming, server functions, bundling, and more.

59
00:05:20,496 --> 00:05:26,736
It's powered by Tanstack Router and Vit, and it's ready to deploy to your favorite hosting provider.

60
00:05:26,990 --> 00:05:32,452
This means Vercel, Netlify, Cloudflare, what else, Hetzner, stuff like that.

61
00:05:32,750 --> 00:05:35,296
But now you might ask me, hey Jan, this sounds good.

62
00:05:35,376 --> 00:05:42,884
So Tanstack Start is a full stack framework, it offers SSR, server-side rendering, streaming, all of the fancy stuff.

63
00:05:43,118 --> 00:05:44,256
But how is it composed?

64
00:05:44,416 --> 00:05:45,856
How is it architected?

65
00:05:46,096 --> 00:05:51,776
Good question, and this is probably one of the most important questions that you can ask right now.

66
00:05:52,078 --> 00:06:02,084
So, right here I created a beautiful diagram, and you should probably already see that 90% of Tanstack Start is just Tanstack Router.

67
00:06:02,256 --> 00:06:04,416
But what even is Tanstack Router?

68
00:06:04,654 --> 00:06:11,460
Well, Tanstack Router is a powerful React router for client-side and full stack React applications.

69
00:06:11,790 --> 00:06:23,936
It offers fully type-safe APIs, first-class search params for managing state in the URL, and seamless integration with the existing React ecosystem, and that's super important.

70
00:06:24,430 --> 00:06:30,724
So Tanstack Start pretty much just gives you server-side rendering, streaming, server functions, and so on.

71
00:06:30,896 --> 00:06:35,136
But primarily what you get with Tanstack Start is Tanstack Router.

72
00:06:35,216 --> 00:06:37,936
And this creates this type-safe experience.

73
00:06:38,190 --> 00:06:42,176
But we will dive into the features of Tanstack Start a bit later on.

74
00:06:42,336 --> 00:06:46,916
And there are 10% still missing because 90% is Tanstack Router.

75
00:06:47,056 --> 00:06:47,812
But what's the rest?

76
00:06:48,576 --> 00:06:50,788
how are the other 10% composed?

77
00:06:51,086 --> 00:06:54,336
Well, there are two tools used, Vit and Nitro.

78
00:06:54,606 --> 00:06:55,428
So what is Vit?

79
00:06:55,536 --> 00:07:03,536
Well, Vit is a modern build tool that provides fast development with hot module replacement and optimized production builds.

80
00:07:03,696 --> 00:07:18,656
And I'm so grateful that the team behind Tanstack Star chose Vit and did not implement their own bundler, I guess with Webpack or ES build, because as you probably all know, Next.js is one of the biggest frameworks in the React ecosystem.

81
00:07:18,862 --> 00:07:20,176
And they use TurboPack.

82
00:07:20,270 --> 00:07:24,016
TurboPack is their own bundler that they created, and it's not bad.

83
00:07:24,096 --> 00:07:29,136
I don't want to badmouth this product, this tool, TurboPack, but Vit is just better.

84
00:07:29,216 --> 00:07:36,736
It's bigger, or I guess it has a bigger history, it has a bigger ecosystem, and just in general, I like Viet a lot more.

85
00:07:36,942 --> 00:07:46,016
So I'm very thankful that they chose Vit and did not architect their own bundler, which would just create a worse experience, it would be slower and stuff like that.

86
00:07:46,256 --> 00:07:50,756
Vit works well, or I mean it works great, it's super fast, and I love it.

87
00:07:50,990 --> 00:07:52,816
And what exactly is Nitro?

88
00:07:53,056 --> 00:07:58,656
Because you might say, I'm familiar with Tanstack Router, I know what Vit is, but what is Nitro?

89
00:07:59,056 --> 00:08:03,456
Well, Nitro is a very interesting product or tool, pretty much.

90
00:08:03,726 --> 00:08:10,496
So Nitro is an open source framework to build web servers using H3 and lots of built-in features.

91
00:08:10,734 --> 00:08:16,932
Nitro automatically makes your code compatible with any deployment provider and runtime.

92
00:08:17,136 --> 00:08:18,336
And that's super important.

93
00:08:18,766 --> 00:08:22,336
So a few weeks ago, I watched an interview with the creator.

94
00:08:22,510 --> 00:08:25,376
So this is Tanner, he created Tanstack Start.

95
00:08:25,742 --> 00:08:31,136
Not he himself, there are also other members, but he is pretty much the face of the Tanstack ecosystem.

96
00:08:31,310 --> 00:08:34,016
And he said, Hey, I want to create a framework.

97
00:08:34,158 --> 00:08:35,616
This means Tanstack Start.

98
00:08:36,048 --> 00:08:49,380
But he also said initially or quite early into development, he realized hey, this might be unmaintainable because I don't want to lock users into a specific deployment provider, let's say Vercel or netlify.

99
00:08:49,568 --> 00:08:51,008
I want to make everything open.

100
00:08:51,182 --> 00:08:58,448
Users should be able to choose any deployment provider Vercel, netlify, I guess Hetzner with Docker or stuff like that.

101
00:08:58,608 --> 00:09:01,328
And not only the deployment provider, but also the runtime.

102
00:09:01,408 --> 00:09:07,088
Because I think you all know there are two main runtimes: the Node.js runtime and the v8 runtime.

103
00:09:07,168 --> 00:09:11,408
And the v8 runtime is mostly also referred to the edge runtime.

104
00:09:11,488 --> 00:09:14,928
This is what, for example, Cloudflare Workers uses in the background.

105
00:09:15,088 --> 00:09:27,808
And the thing is, if the team behind Tanstack Star chose to do everything by themselves, this means to create all plugins, so plugins for every deployment provider, and just to maintain all of the code, then this would not be maintainable.

106
00:09:27,968 --> 00:09:32,128
But thankfully, they chose Nitro, and Nitro is a very universal tool.

107
00:09:32,288 --> 00:09:39,556
This means you can deploy Nitro anywhere Netlify, vercel you can choose the Node.js runtime, the Edge runtime.

108
00:09:39,728 --> 00:09:42,688
It does not really matter, and that's very important.

109
00:09:43,150 --> 00:09:48,688
So if you go to the documentation and then search for, I think hosting, then you will find this section.

110
00:09:48,768 --> 00:09:50,928
And here they also reiterate the same thing.

111
00:09:51,118 --> 00:09:56,608
You can deploy your application to Cloudflare workers Netlify, which is the official partner.

112
00:09:56,910 --> 00:10:08,128
You can use Nitro then to deploy your application to Vercel or, for example, to any other Node.js hosting provider, so Railway or by using Docker and then Hetzner and stuff like that.

113
00:10:08,288 --> 00:10:11,648
So in general, the nice thing is that this is completely universal.

114
00:10:11,888 --> 00:10:16,448
You are not locked in into any provider as with, for example, other frameworks.

115
00:10:16,558 --> 00:10:18,116
