00:25:09,326 --> 00:25:18,512
And inside of here they already defined a new router instance and also configured the router by, for example, setting scroll restoration to true.

342
00:25:18,672 --> 00:25:26,276
And you can configure literally everything in this file, as they mentioned here, from preloading functionality to caching staleness.

343
00:25:26,512 --> 00:25:27,712
Literally everything.

344
00:25:27,950 --> 00:25:30,404
And now let's continue with routing itself.

345
00:25:30,766 --> 00:25:35,140
So what you have to understand is that there are two main ways to implement routing.

346
00:25:35,392 --> 00:25:40,580
There is file-based routing, and you can also, in theory, define routes using code.

347
00:25:40,846 --> 00:25:49,952
But I would always recommend you to use file-based routing because, in my opinion, file-based routing is more scalable and also easier to understand.

348
00:25:50,190 --> 00:25:55,312
And most frameworks that I worked with in the past always used file-based routing.

349
00:25:55,598 --> 00:25:58,192
Tanstack Start offers file-based routing.

350
00:25:58,432 --> 00:26:00,992
Next.js offers file-based routing.

351
00:26:01,152 --> 00:26:06,112
I guess React Router V7 and with that remix also offer file-based routing.

352
00:26:06,272 --> 00:26:10,052
In my opinion, it's the best abstraction to create routes.

353
00:26:10,382 --> 00:26:19,712
And as they say here, start uses tanstack router's file-based routing approach to ensure proper code splitting and advanced type safety.

354
00:26:19,886 --> 00:26:23,232
You can find your routes in the routes directory.

355
00:26:23,406 --> 00:26:26,752
That's the directory, the folder I showed you a second ago.

356
00:26:26,912 --> 00:26:28,612
And let's start with the root route.

357
00:26:28,752 --> 00:26:32,752
This is this underscore underscore root.tsx file.

358
00:26:32,942 --> 00:26:33,872
What is this file?

359
00:26:34,062 --> 00:26:34,912
What does it do?

360
00:26:35,072 --> 00:26:37,444
Well, this is actually the root route.

361
00:26:37,678 --> 00:26:44,832
And the root route is the topmost route in the entire tree and encapsulates all other routes as children.

362
00:26:44,912 --> 00:26:48,592
And it must be named underscore underscore root.txt.

363
00:26:49,072 --> 00:26:52,672
So as they say here, it has no path and is always matched.

364
00:26:52,832 --> 00:26:54,912
Its component is always rendered.

365
00:26:55,086 --> 00:26:59,632
This is where you render your document shell, example HTML body, etc.

366
00:27:00,046 --> 00:27:07,780
And because it's always rendered, it's the perfect place to construct your application shell and take care of any global logic.

367
00:27:07,982 --> 00:27:10,832
And I know exactly what you want to say right now.

368
00:27:10,992 --> 00:27:13,552
Hey Jan, this sounds super confusing.

369
00:27:13,806 --> 00:27:21,028
So it encapsulates all other routes as children, it does not have any path, it has to always be rendered.

370
00:27:21,632 --> 00:27:22,112
What?

371
00:27:22,286 --> 00:27:23,632
What does this route do?

372
00:27:23,792 --> 00:27:24,832
Why is it there?

373
00:27:25,134 --> 00:27:28,004
So let me try to break it down with simple terms.

374
00:27:28,462 --> 00:27:29,472
So imagine this.

375
00:27:29,632 --> 00:27:32,912
This route is the entry point to your application.

376
00:27:33,134 --> 00:27:41,316
This means whenever you visit any route, your dashboard route, your hello route, your ABC route, then this is the initial route that gets rendered.

377
00:27:41,552 --> 00:27:50,352
And in this route, they for example added the HTML tag, the head tag, and also, for example, meta tags, and then the header component.

378
00:27:50,512 --> 00:27:55,392
The header component is like the nav bar that they created right here, and then they render children.

379
00:27:55,598 --> 00:27:57,572
And the children are all of your routes.

380
00:27:57,838 --> 00:28:04,592
So as mentioned later on, for example, we will create a dashboard route and then a dashboard slash import route.

381
00:28:04,672 --> 00:28:07,792
So the import route will be the child route of the dashboard route.

382
00:28:07,886 --> 00:28:12,580
So we will create multiple routes and they will be children of this root route.

383
00:28:12,782 --> 00:28:16,832
This means whatever you render here will also be rendered in all of your other routes.

384
00:28:17,230 --> 00:28:20,864
So this header, for example, is you could almost say static.

385
00:28:20,944 --> 00:28:22,624
It will always be on the top.

386
00:28:22,784 --> 00:28:25,092
It does not matter what route you visit.

387
00:28:25,504 --> 00:28:27,904
So let me show it to you with a diagram.

388
00:28:28,078 --> 00:28:30,864
So imagine the following: this is your index page.

389
00:28:30,944 --> 00:28:32,944
This means localhost 3000.

390
00:28:33,294 --> 00:28:34,704
And this is your about page.

391
00:28:34,798 --> 00:28:37,584
So localhost 3000 slash about.

392
00:28:38,144 --> 00:28:39,600
You now have different content.

393
00:28:39,824 --> 00:28:45,344
So right here, for example, it says hello index page, and here it says hello about page.

394
00:28:45,584 --> 00:28:47,584
But you also have your root route.

395
00:28:47,664 --> 00:28:49,380
It's pretty much like the root layout.

396
00:28:49,614 --> 00:28:51,504
And inside of here you render your header.

397
00:28:51,664 --> 00:28:56,292
And this header, so this one on the top, the navbar, will be persisted on all routes.

398
00:28:56,624 --> 00:29:02,064
So if you navigate to the about route, the navbar will stay as is and it won't re-render.

399
00:29:02,414 --> 00:29:03,344
That's very important.

400
00:29:03,694 --> 00:29:09,252
So whatever gets rendered here, for example, the header in this case, won't re-render on navigation.

401
00:29:09,584 --> 00:29:10,624
That's very cool.

402
00:29:10,704 --> 00:29:13,904
And I will also dive into re-renders a bit later on.

403
00:29:14,158 --> 00:29:17,156
And inside of here, they then render 10 stack dev tools.

