00:34:38,032 --> 00:34:42,352
And let's also delete the constant features because we don't need this array anymore.

481
00:34:42,542 --> 00:34:45,552
I will delete the imports, we also don't need them anymore.

482
00:34:45,742 --> 00:34:50,532
And if I save this and go back again, then you will see right here hello world.

483
00:34:50,752 --> 00:34:52,912
And we can also update our header.

484
00:34:53,072 --> 00:35:01,124
So if you go back to the root, we currently render this header component above the children, and that's why you also see the header above hello world.

485
00:35:01,326 --> 00:35:08,324
If you would, for example, render this header underneath the children, then you would first of all see hello world and then the header.

486
00:35:08,526 --> 00:35:11,632
So pretty much again, the children are all of your routes.

487
00:35:11,712 --> 00:35:17,220
And then whatever you render above or below will then be rendered above your children or below your children.

488
00:35:17,486 --> 00:35:19,876
So let's render the header above the children.

489
00:35:20,112 --> 00:35:22,244
And I will also open the component.

490
00:35:22,352 --> 00:35:30,352
So in the source folder, you will find a components folder with the header file, and I will delete everything in the return statement.

491
00:35:30,816 --> 00:35:40,976
And inside of here, I will just say, I guess, h1 hello from the header component rendered in the root route.

492
00:35:41,216 --> 00:35:43,936
If I save this and go back, you will see this right here.

493
00:35:44,016 --> 00:35:47,524
So hello from the header component rendered in the root route.

494
00:35:47,726 --> 00:35:51,296
So I think you now finally understand how the root route works.

495
00:35:51,456 --> 00:35:57,456
Nevertheless, you might say, hey Jan, okay, so the root route is the entry point to my application.

496
00:35:57,646 --> 00:35:59,776
I render all of my children inside of here.

497
00:35:59,936 --> 00:36:03,428
The children are all of the routes, but how do I create routes?

498
00:36:03,616 --> 00:36:10,176
Like we have this index route, but how do you create a test route or an ABC route or an hello route?

499
00:36:10,478 --> 00:36:12,804
Like literally, how do you create routes?

500
00:36:13,136 --> 00:36:13,856
Good question.

501
00:36:14,158 --> 00:36:23,140
So please again head over to the Tanstack router documentation, not to Tanstack Start, and inside of here you will find a section called basic routes.

502
00:36:23,376 --> 00:36:25,764
Basic routes match a specific path.

503
00:36:25,936 --> 00:36:29,776
For example, slash about slash settings and so on.

504
00:36:29,902 --> 00:36:33,604
And they are all basic routes as they match the path exactly.

505
00:36:33,838 --> 00:36:35,812
Let's take a look at an about route.

506
00:36:36,078 --> 00:36:42,176
So in the about route, you use the create file route function, and then you literally just return your content.

507
00:36:42,336 --> 00:36:43,296
Let's test it out.

508
00:36:43,566 --> 00:36:53,380
So I will go back, open my explorer, and then in the source folder and in the routes folder, I will create a new file called about.tsx.

509
00:36:53,774 --> 00:36:57,216
And inside of here, it automatically generated this code.

510
00:36:57,454 --> 00:37:01,776
So as you see here, you don't need to define the create file route function.

511
00:37:01,856 --> 00:37:09,296
You don't need to link your component to the component itself, or you don't need to link the file route function to your component.

512
00:37:09,376 --> 00:37:11,056
Everything is done automatically.

513
00:37:11,296 --> 00:37:15,456
This is the power of Tanstack Router, and with that, Tanstack start.

514
00:37:15,616 --> 00:37:22,656
And please make sure that your dev server is also running, because only if your dev server is running, it will also auto-generate this code.

515
00:37:22,926 --> 00:37:26,052
So let's go back and I will try to visit the new route.

516
00:37:26,318 --> 00:37:36,256
So in the URL, let's say slash about, and as you see here, we still see the header, hello from the header component, but then we see our new content, hello about.

517
00:37:36,814 --> 00:37:43,856
So if I go back to the index page again, we still have our header, but the main content updated because we see hello world.

518
00:37:43,936 --> 00:37:48,804
And that's what I tried to explain to you right here with this diagram or with this example.

519
00:37:49,006 --> 00:37:57,776
The main content changes with every route, but everything that is rendered in your route does not re-render, it stays as is, and that's very powerful.

520
00:37:57,936 --> 00:37:58,736
And you know what?

521
00:37:58,816 --> 00:38:01,456
Let's create another route just to test things out.

522
00:38:01,616 --> 00:38:06,052
In the routes folder, again, you want to create all of the routes in the routes folder.

523
00:38:06,256 --> 00:38:12,452
I will create another file and this will be called demo or not demo, let's say yo.tsx.

524
00:38:12,896 --> 00:38:17,696
If I now just click on enter, it will again auto-generate the code for me.

525
00:38:17,856 --> 00:38:19,216
I don't have to do anything.

526
00:38:19,376 --> 00:38:28,576
And if I copy the name yo and go back and then navigate to yo, so slash yo, I will still see the header, the nav bar, but my content changed.

527
00:38:28,736 --> 00:38:29,616
That's very nice.

528
00:38:29,838 --> 00:38:32,416
But now you might say, okay, Jen, this all makes sense.

529
00:38:32,496 --> 00:38:37,536
So I now know how to create basic routes, but how can I navigate a user?

530
00:38:37,776 --> 00:38:43,376
Because no real user will go to the URL and then say slash yo or slash dashboard.

531
00:38:43,616 --> 00:38:46,896
No, the user wants to click on a button to get redirected.

532
00:38:47,056 --> 00:38:49,284
How about we just use the a tag, right?

533
00:38:49,456 --> 00:38:50,596
So let's test it out.

534
00:38:50,830 --> 00:38:53,936
I will go back to my index.tsx file.

535
00:38:54,096 --> 00:38:57,056
So underneath the h1, let's create an a tag.

536
00:38:57,216 --> 00:39:01,508
And for the href this will be slash, what was the route again about, right?

537
00:39:01,806 --> 00:39:07,376
So for the href this will be slash about, and for the content itself, this will also be about link.

538
00:39:07,790 --> 00:39:10,816
If I now save this and go back, we get this UI.

539
00:39:10,990 --> 00:39:13,376
I will also create some padding to see something.

540
00:39:13,518 --> 00:39:15,268
So class name padding 10.

541
00:39:15,456 --> 00:39:18,736
And if I go back again, then you will see this link right here.

542
00:39:18,896 --> 00:39:21,508
And if I click on it, I will get redirected.

543
00:39:21,742 --> 00:39:24,356
So now you might say, nice, we are finished, right?

544
00:39:24,656 --> 00:39:25,616
No, not really.

545
00:39:25,776 --> 00:39:28,068
I want you to look at this Fav icon.

546
00:39:28,302 --> 00:39:33,188
So in a second, I will click on about link and you should look at the Fav icon on the top.

547
00:39:33,296 --> 00:39:36,176
So one, two, three, and did you see this right here?

548
00:39:36,398 --> 00:39:37,316
The Fav icon spun.

549
00:39:38,096 --> 00:39:38,896
Why is that?

550
00:39:39,150 --> 00:39:45,376
Well, that's because this was not a client-side navigation, but rather how I call it a hard navigation.

551
00:39:45,582 --> 00:39:48,496
This means pretty much the routed a full-on refresh.

552
00:39:48,654 --> 00:39:53,216
It's very similar to if you would just go to the URL and update it yourself.

553
00:39:53,390 --> 00:39:57,616
And this is not very good because this means your full route has to re-render.

554
00:39:57,856 --> 00:39:59,056
And this is not what you want.

555
00:39:59,216 --> 00:40:05,508
Because the power of nested routing is that certain sections don't re-render, like for example, this header.

556
00:40:05,696 --> 00:40:15,536
But if I do a full-on hard navigation as we did a second ago, then the full page re-renders and we lose all of the benefits of nested routing, which is not good.

557
00:40:15,822 --> 00:40:20,176
So is there any way to create a client-side navigation, you might ask me?

558
00:40:20,398 --> 00:40:22,096
Yes, thankfully there is.

559
00:40:22,336 --> 00:40:29,696
If you go back to the Tan Stack Router documentation and search for link component, then you will find this section.

560
00:40:29,902 --> 00:40:38,816
And inside of here they say the link component is the component that can be used to create a link that can be used to navigate to a new location.

561
00:40:39,056 --> 00:40:44,164
This includes changes to the path name, search params, hash, and location state.

562
00:40:44,526 --> 00:40:55,268
Now I will be honest with you, I'm not the biggest fan of the TanStack router documentation because they don't even mention right here that this is type-safe, that this does a client-side navigation and stuff like that.

563
00:40:55,536 --> 00:40:59,136
But in general, this is way more powerful than the normal ATAG.

564
00:40:59,470 --> 00:41:05,924
The ATAG does a hard navigation, there is no client-side navigation, it's not type safe, nothing like that.

565
00:41:06,222 --> 00:41:09,376
So let me show you the benefit of using the link component.

566
00:41:09,616 --> 00:41:13,536
I will delete the A tag and instead I will get the link component.

567
00:41:13,774 --> 00:41:20,516
Please make sure that you import the link component from Tanstack React Router and not from Lucid React.

568
00:41:20,942 --> 00:41:24,256
So I will import it from Tanstack and I will also close it.

569
00:41:24,336 --> 00:41:29,860
And the important thing is instead of using the href property, you want to use the to property.

570
00:41:30,254 --> 00:41:34,084
So I will say to and with that I don't get any errors anymore.

571
00:41:34,286 --> 00:41:41,776
So if I go back and go to my index route and click on about link, you will see right here that this wasn't a hard navigation.

572
00:41:41,966 --> 00:41:44,016
This means the Fav icon did not spin.

573
00:41:44,176 --> 00:41:45,856
Let me show it to you one more time.

574
00:41:46,062 --> 00:41:48,896
I will click on about link and look at the Fav icon.

575
00:41:49,006 --> 00:41:51,296
So one, two, three, bum, did it spin?

576
00:41:51,536 --> 00:41:51,776
No.

577
00:41:51,936 --> 00:41:54,576
And that's because this was a client-side navigation.

578
00:41:54,736 --> 00:41:58,308
And this also means this content on the top did not re-render.

579
00:41:58,496 --> 00:42:03,492
It was just there, it just stayed where it was, and only the main content re-rendered.

580
00:42:03,696 --> 00:42:06,116
That's what you call nested routing.

581
00:42:06,336 --> 00:42:12,228
And you know what the benefit or the cool thing about this link component is, it's completely type safe.

582
00:42:12,526 --> 00:42:16,804
So if I again use the A tag, I can pass any href right here.

583
00:42:16,896 --> 00:42:18,576
So slash some gibberish.

584
00:42:18,816 --> 00:42:20,096
This route does not exist.

585
00:42:20,256 --> 00:42:22,976
And if I save this and go back, you will see this link.

586
00:42:23,056 --> 00:42:27,588
And if I click on it, I will get an error because this route does not exist.

587
00:42:27,886 --> 00:42:30,148
But the link component is type safe.

588
00:42:30,286 --> 00:42:37,348
So if I add some gibberish, it will instantly throw an error because it says, Hey Jan, this route does not exist.

589
00:42:37,536 --> 00:42:38,976
What are you trying to do?

590
00:42:39,216 --> 00:42:43,684
And if I click on control spacebar, I get access to all of my routes.

591
00:42:43,950 --> 00:42:50,016
So these are all of the routes that currently exist the index route, slash about, yo, and so on.

592
00:42:50,286 --> 00:42:54,816
So if I select about, I don't get any errors, and that's because this route exists.

593
00:42:55,248 --> 00:42:57,968
And I will also explain how this works in a second.

594
00:42:58,158 --> 00:43:01,568
Let's maybe create one more link, this time for the Yo route.

595
00:43:01,728 --> 00:43:06,628
So if I click on Control Spacebar, I instantly get my types, yo or autocomplete.

596
00:43:06,926 --> 00:43:10,692
So if I go back and click on Yo, it instantly redirects me.

597
00:43:10,928 --> 00:43:11,968
That's super cool.

598
00:43:12,174 --> 00:43:21,540
But now you might ask me, as already mentioned, hey Jan, how is Tanstack Start or how can Tanstack Router even create such a type safe experience?

599
00:43:21,774 --> 00:43:28,768
Because if you, for example, already used Next.js, then you will know that Next.js also offers a link component.

600
00:43:28,928 --> 00:43:33,648
But the link component offered by Next.js, for example, is not TypeSafe.

601
00:43:33,838 --> 00:43:37,168
Why is the link component type safe in TANStack Start?

602
00:43:37,488 --> 00:43:45,168
Well, that's because if you open your Explorer, you will find a file called routetree.gen, so generated.ts.

603
00:43:45,614 --> 00:43:49,568
So this file gets automatically generated by TanStack Router.

604
00:43:49,648 --> 00:43:52,288
And you should not make any changes to this file.

605
00:43:52,462 --> 00:43:59,168
And this file literally, whenever you start your dev server, analyzes all of the routes and adds them inside of here.

606
00:43:59,374 --> 00:44:01,892
So that's why the link component is type safe.

607
00:44:02,094 --> 00:44:06,884
The link component references this file and with that references all of the routes.

608
00:44:07,150 --> 00:44:20,836
And if a route is not added or does not exist in this file, then the link component throws an error because it says, hey Jan, I have access to the route tree file, but the route tree file does not hold such a route.

609
00:44:21,008 --> 00:44:25,168
This means this route does not exist and I should probably throw an error.

610
00:44:25,328 --> 00:44:31,968
And that's super powerful and something that I haven't seen with any other frameworks, to be quite honest with you.

611
00:44:32,128 --> 00:44:34,768
And that's what you will also see in the documentation.

612
00:44:34,926 --> 00:44:43,408
So if you go to the Tanstack Start documentation, they say right here, you may notice a route tree.gen.ts file in your project.

613
00:44:43,648 --> 00:44:51,408
This file is automatically generated when you run tanstack start via npm rundev or pnpm rundev and so on.

614
00:44:51,598 --> 00:45:02,928
And this file contains the generated route tree and a handful of TypeScript utilities that make Tanstack Start's type safety extremely fast and fully inferred.

615
00:45:03,088 --> 00:45:05,168
And trust me, this is a game changer.

616
00:45:05,328 --> 00:45:16,848
Now you might say, okay Jen, this fully makes sense, and I now know how to create basic routes, I know how to navigate users, I also fully understand what the root route is and what it does.

617
00:45:17,088 --> 00:45:18,820
But how can I nest routes?

618
00:45:19,008 --> 00:45:23,888
Because in a production rate application, you will at some point need to nest routes.

619
00:45:23,968 --> 00:45:33,124
As an example, slash dashboard slash settings, slash dashboard, slash billing, slash dashboard slash import, and so on.

620
00:45:33,358 --> 00:45:35,108
So you will want to nest routes.

621
00:45:35,408 --> 00:45:36,448
But how do you do that?

622
00:45:36,688 --> 00:45:37,408
Good question.

623
00:45:37,678 --> 00:45:43,168
Please head over to the documentation and search for this section, file-based routing.

624
00:45:43,328 --> 00:45:47,876
Inside of here, they again explain what file-based routing is and stuff like that.

625
00:45:48,048 --> 00:45:58,688
And inside of here they also say while file-based routing is the preferred and recommended way to configure TanStack router, you can also use code-based routing.

626
00:45:58,926 --> 00:46:01,968
So this means you can define all of your routes using code.

627
00:46:02,208 --> 00:46:04,768
I will be honest with you, I would not recommend that.

628
00:46:04,928 --> 00:46:11,008
In my opinion, this just makes things more complex and it's not that scalable, in my humble opinion.

629
00:46:11,168 --> 00:46:14,128
I mean, if you disagree with me, then please let me know.

630
00:46:14,254 --> 00:46:22,564
But humbly, I would say that file-based routing is the better way to configure routes in Tanstack Start and with that Tanstack Router.

631
00:46:22,830 --> 00:46:26,608
Here they again mention what file-based routing is and what the benefits are.

632
00:46:26,768 --> 00:46:33,728
In short, simplicity, organization, scalability, code splitting, type safety, and consistency.

633
00:46:33,888 --> 00:46:42,208
And now you might think, okay, so file based routing, this means there's probably just one way to implement file based routing, right?

634
00:46:42,528 --> 00:46:44,452
No, actually that's incorrect.

635
00:46:44,768 --> 00:46:49,248
There are two ways you can use directory routes or flat routes.

636
00:46:49,774 --> 00:46:52,848
So, what's the difference and what does that even mean?

637
00:46:53,166 --> 00:47:05,888
So, while directories have long been used to represent route hierarchy, file-based routing introduces an additional concept of using the dot character in the file name to denote a route nesting.

638
00:47:06,048 --> 00:47:15,364
This allows you to avoid creating directories for few deeply nested routes and continue to use directories for wider route hierarchies.

639
00:47:15,726 --> 00:47:18,276
So let's first of all look at directory routes.

640
00:47:18,368 --> 00:47:20,768
And by the way, a directory is a folder.

641
00:47:21,008 --> 00:47:33,860
Directories can be used to denote route hierarchy, which can be useful for organizing multiple routes into logical groups and also cutting down on the file name length for large groups of deeply nested routes.

642
00:47:34,048 --> 00:47:37,328
In this example, they've created a posts folder.

643
00:47:37,488 --> 00:47:44,128
And this posts folder has two files, an index.tsx file and a post id file.

644
00:47:44,368 --> 00:47:53,328
The index file literally maps to slash posts, and the postID route maps to slash posts, slash post ID.

645
00:47:53,488 --> 00:47:54,948
So this is a dynamic route.

646
00:47:55,182 --> 00:48:06,128
And if you would, for example, want to render an about route, you could either create an about.tsx file or in about folder with an index.tsx file.

647
00:48:06,288 --> 00:48:08,128
This will also create then the route.

648
00:48:08,288 --> 00:48:10,528
So slash posts slash about.

649
00:48:11,168 --> 00:48:15,168
And you can also use file or flat routes as mentioned.

650
00:48:15,248 --> 00:48:20,388
And flat routes give you the ability to use dots to denote route nesting levels.

651
00:48:20,782 --> 00:48:29,728
This can be useful when you have a large number of uniquely deeply nested routes and want to avoid creating directories for each one.

652
00:48:29,934 --> 00:48:33,156
So as an example, let's again look at the posts route.

653
00:48:33,248 --> 00:48:38,848
They right here have a posts file and then a posts.index.tsx file.

654
00:48:39,054 --> 00:48:44,932
So this is an exact match and this will then in the URL pretty much map to slash posts.

655
00:48:45,248 --> 00:48:48,128
But now you want to, for example, nest a dynamic route.

656
00:48:48,208 --> 00:48:49,028
How do you do that?

657
00:48:49,328 --> 00:48:58,756
Well, you use a dot, so posts.postid, or if you want to nest it even further, posts.postid.edit, and so on.

658
00:48:59,022 --> 00:49:01,604
So you can either use directories or dots.

659
00:49:01,808 --> 00:49:03,648
I will be super honest with you.

660
00:49:03,968 --> 00:49:08,048
I probably use directories 90% of the time.

661
00:49:08,302 --> 00:49:13,488
I'm not the biggest fan of flat routes because they also create a bit of funky business.

662
00:49:13,648 --> 00:49:22,288
Okay, this sounds a bit weird, but if you don't fully understand them, they can be a bit complicated because they also create layouts and sometimes you don't really realize it.

663
00:49:22,542 --> 00:49:32,772
So, in my opinion, I just like directories more, especially since I'm an XJS developer, primarily, and this is just how I like to create my routes.

664
00:49:33,168 --> 00:49:35,248
But you can also mix and match.

665
00:49:35,470 --> 00:49:37,892
So mixed flat and directory routes.

666
00:49:38,222 --> 00:49:56,996
It's extremely likely that a 100% directory or flat route structure won't be the best fit for your project, which is why Tanstack Router allows you to mix both flat and directory routes together to create a route tree that uses the best of both worlds where it makes sense.

667
00:49:57,248 --> 00:50:04,768
As an example, you can create a posts folder, then with an index.tsx file, and this will create the exact match.

668
00:50:04,848 --> 00:50:06,148
This means slash posts.

669
00:50:06,608 --> 00:50:12,928
And if you want to have another dynamic route, so a child route, you can create another file, post ID.

670
00:50:13,038 --> 00:50:16,288
So this is what we saw in the directory example.

671
00:50:16,462 --> 00:50:23,396
But let's say you want to create another child route, so slash posts, slash post id, slash edit.

672
00:50:23,648 --> 00:50:30,276
Instead of creating another folder called edit and then index.tsx, you literally can just use a dot.

673
00:50:30,478 --> 00:50:35,652
And since this is a very simple, you could say nested structure, this also makes sense.

674
00:50:36,110 --> 00:50:40,840
So what they try to say is hey, if you have a lot of nested routes, use directory.

675
00:50:41,168 --> 00:50:47,168
So if you, for example, have a post route and this post route has hundreds of nested routes, use a folder.

676
00:50:47,328 --> 00:50:48,612
This will make a bit more sense.

677
00:50:48,928 --> 00:51:01,488
But if you have some sort of route, like for example, post ID, and this post ID route has only one child route, then it might not really make sense to create a directory and instead just create a this dot.

678
00:51:01,648 --> 00:51:03,648
Or yeah, just use flat routes.

679
00:51:03,728 --> 00:51:04,848
That's what I'm trying to say.

680
00:51:05,008 --> 00:51:09,248
And this currently might confuse you, so don't worry about it, I will show it to you.

681
00:51:09,454 --> 00:51:11,968
So let's go back to our application.

682
00:51:12,142 --> 00:51:15,088
And in the routes folder, you will find a demo folder.

683
00:51:15,214 --> 00:51:19,888
And these are demo routes that have been created when we created our project.

684
00:51:20,142 --> 00:51:21,860
And here they use flat routes.

685
00:51:22,062 --> 00:51:30,848
This means start.api-request.tsx, start.server, start.ssr, start.ssr.full and so on.

686
00:51:31,022 --> 00:51:36,128
Again, I'm not the biggest fan of flat routes, I feel like they are a bit confusing at the start.

687
00:51:36,288 --> 00:51:41,636
Therefore, in this video, we will use directories and whenever it makes sense, we will use flat routes.

688
00:51:42,094 --> 00:51:43,812
So let's delete the demo folder.

689
00:51:43,904 --> 00:51:47,012
And I want to now show you how to create nested routes.

690
00:51:47,470 --> 00:51:49,104
So what routes should we create?

691
00:51:49,584 --> 00:51:51,584
Let's right here just write it out.

692
00:51:51,744 --> 00:51:54,704
I will make this a bit bigger so that you can see something.

693
00:51:54,862 --> 00:52:06,340
So let's say we want to have a posts route, then I want to have a slash post slash post ID route, and then a slash post slash post ID slash edit route.

694
00:52:06,574 --> 00:52:09,092
So in total we want to create three routes.

695
00:52:09,422 --> 00:52:10,820
And yeah, let's test it out.

696
00:52:11,024 --> 00:52:16,324
So let's go back and in the routes folder I will create a new folder called posts.

697
00:52:16,654 --> 00:52:20,944
And in the posts folder, I will create an index.tsx file.

698
00:52:21,104 --> 00:52:23,904
This will automatically generate the route content.

699
00:52:24,046 --> 00:52:32,356
And if I save this and go back and then navigate to slash post, I will say slash posts, then you will see right here the header and then hello posts.

700
00:52:32,624 --> 00:52:33,904
Let's now nest a route.

701
00:52:34,064 --> 00:52:38,224
Instead of nesting this post ID route, which is dynamic, let's do something else.

702
00:52:38,384 --> 00:52:40,516
Let's create maybe a test route.

703
00:52:40,750 --> 00:52:44,064
So in posts, I will create a new folder called test.

704
00:52:44,398 --> 00:52:48,704
And in the test folder, I will create an index.tsx file.

705
00:52:48,942 --> 00:52:50,544
This will again generate the code.

706
00:52:50,702 --> 00:52:59,984
And if I go back and then navigate to slash posts slash test, you will see right here that we now have a nested route and the content also updated.

707
00:53:00,174 --> 00:53:01,584
We can nest it even further.

708
00:53:01,744 --> 00:53:07,024
In the test folder, I can create a yo folder with an index.tsx file.

709
00:53:07,184 --> 00:53:15,504
And if I save this and go back and navigate to slash yo, so slash post slash test slash yo, it also updated everything.

710
00:53:15,694 --> 00:53:18,788
So this is how you create basic nested routes.

711
00:53:19,104 --> 00:53:21,476
In theory, you could also use flat routes.

712
00:53:21,806 --> 00:53:29,124
So I could now say, hey, in the yo route, I want to create another route, and this will be called hello.tsx.

713
00:53:29,390 --> 00:53:37,824
So if I save this and go back and then navigate to slash posts slash test slash yo slash hello, this will also work.

714
00:53:38,030 --> 00:53:41,904
But again, in my opinion, this just makes everything a bit more confusing.

715
00:53:42,094 --> 00:53:45,796
So I like to use directories instead of flat routes.

716
00:53:45,998 --> 00:53:49,344
And let's now delete this test folder with the yo folder.

717
00:53:49,424 --> 00:53:52,100
And how do you now create a dynamic route?

718
00:53:52,334 --> 00:53:59,300
Because inside of here in this example, I said, hey, I want you have a posts route and then a post slash post ID route.

719
00:53:59,470 --> 00:54:02,116
And the post ID route has to be dynamic.

720
00:54:02,384 --> 00:54:03,904
Well, it's relatively simple.

721
00:54:04,110 --> 00:54:05,956
You want to use the dollar syntax.

722
00:54:06,286 --> 00:54:13,744
So in this example, if you scroll a bit to the top, they used this dollar symbol, and this dollar symbol creates a dynamic route.

723
00:54:13,902 --> 00:54:18,944
So in the posts folder, I will create another folder, and this will be called post ID.

724
00:54:19,264 --> 00:54:23,664
The important thing is you want to prefix the folder name with a dollar symbol.

725
00:54:23,822 --> 00:54:27,584
And inside of here I will create an index.tsx file.

726
00:54:27,744 --> 00:54:34,544
If I save this and go back and do a hard refresh, I currently get an error, and that's because I need to update the URL.

727
00:54:34,766 --> 00:54:38,224
So this will now be slash posts slash some gibberish.

728
00:54:38,318 --> 00:54:42,864
And if I click on enter, I see right here hello posts slash post ID.

729
00:54:43,214 --> 00:54:47,664
And since this post ID route is a dynamic route, I can pass any gibberish.

730
00:54:47,824 --> 00:54:58,704
So if you for example visit my application and click on a tutorial, you will also see that this is a dynamic path slash free tutorials slash the name of the tutorial.

731
00:54:58,830 --> 00:55:00,144
And that's what we did right here.

732
00:55:00,304 --> 00:55:01,744
But this is now the post ID.

733
00:55:01,904 --> 00:55:03,940
But currently we don't render the post ID.

734
00:55:04,064 --> 00:55:10,624
And you might ask me, hey Jen, how can I get the post ID from the URL and rendered inside of here?

735
00:55:10,864 --> 00:55:11,664
Good question.

736
00:55:11,918 --> 00:55:17,744
So if you head over to the documentation and go to Tanstack Router and click on routing concepts.

737
00:55:18,176 --> 00:55:21,380
You will find a section called dynamic route segments.

738
00:55:21,646 --> 00:55:34,656
And inside of here they say route path segments that start with a dollar sign followed by a label are dynamic and capture that section of the URL in the params object for use in your application.

739
00:55:34,816 --> 00:55:46,084
For example, a path name of posts slash one two three would match the posts slash post ID route and the params object would be post ID equals to 123.

740
00:55:46,576 --> 00:55:48,976
And how can you get access to this param?

741
00:55:49,230 --> 00:55:52,816
Well you can use the route itself and then the use params hook.

742
00:55:52,910 --> 00:56:02,576
So if we go back to the application, you can right here say constant, destructure it, and then this will be equal to route, uppercase route.use params.

743
00:56:02,896 --> 00:56:07,684
And if I invoke it and click on control spacebar, I instantly get my params.

744
00:56:07,950 --> 00:56:10,276
And the param is called post ID.

745
00:56:10,734 --> 00:56:12,336
So let's render the post ID.

746
00:56:12,416 --> 00:56:18,496
I will delete this post slash post ID and instead I will render the dynamic post ID.

747
00:56:18,638 --> 00:56:22,656
And if I go back, you will see right here hello and then this dynamic param.

748
00:56:22,976 --> 00:56:29,856
If I update it to something normal, dynamic underscore ID underscore param, then you will also see it right here.

749
00:56:30,030 --> 00:56:34,096
So this is how easy it is to access all of your dynamic params.

750
00:56:34,416 --> 00:56:42,976
For example, if you use Nextjs or if you used Next.js in the past, then you will know that this is not TypeSafe in Next.js.

751
00:56:43,502 --> 00:56:49,616
But since TanStack Router is super smart and fully type safe, the params are also type safe.

752
00:56:49,776 --> 00:56:55,524
If I add some gibberish right here, I instantly get an error, and that's because this param does not exist.

753
00:56:55,856 --> 00:57:02,096
The file is called post ID, and this also means the dynamic param has to be called post ID.

754
00:57:02,190 --> 00:57:12,016
And that's again because we have this route tree file and it analyzes all of the routes and it sees hey, the dynamic param is called post ID and not some other gibberish.

755
00:57:12,206 --> 00:57:15,936
So let's close this again and we want to nest one more route.

756
00:57:16,078 --> 00:57:17,156
So the edit route.

757
00:57:17,456 --> 00:57:21,936
Let's go back and in the post id folder, I will create another folder.

758
00:57:22,126 --> 00:57:27,936
This will be called edit, and in the edit folder, I will create an index.tsx file.

759
00:57:28,110 --> 00:57:36,816
This means if I save this and go back and then navigate to slash posts slash some gibberish slash edit, I will see this route right here.

760
00:57:36,976 --> 00:57:39,908
And I can still access the params in this route.

761
00:57:40,238 --> 00:57:51,296
So I can just go above the return statement, constant, I will destructure this, and this will then be equal to route dot use params, not use loader data but use params.

762
00:57:51,856 --> 00:57:57,056
I will invoke it, and then where I destructured everything, I can access the post ID.

763
00:57:57,198 --> 00:57:58,816
I will render it inside of here.

764
00:57:58,976 --> 00:58:03,456
This means if I save this and go back, I will see my post ID inside of here.

765
00:58:03,616 --> 00:58:06,096
Hello AS something something something.

766
00:58:06,256 --> 00:58:14,176
If I make this something normal, like I guess ID underscore one two three, I will also see right here hello id123.

767
00:58:14,574 --> 00:58:19,492
So this is how you can nest routes and also how you can create dynamic routes.

768
00:58:19,856 --> 00:58:23,376
And in theory, you don't even have to create the edit folder.

769
00:58:23,470 --> 00:58:27,216
So if I delete this edit folder, I will also remove the edit route.

770
00:58:27,296 --> 00:58:29,296
I will instantly get an error right here.

771
00:58:29,518 --> 00:58:32,336
That's because slash edit does not exist anymore.

772
00:58:32,526 --> 00:58:41,376
And in theory, instead of creating a folder, I can just create a file in the post id folder, call it edit.tsx, and it will do the same thing.

773
00:58:41,582 --> 00:58:44,656
So if I do a hard refresh, I again see my content.

774
00:58:44,816 --> 00:58:47,856
This is again what you call flat-based routes.

775
00:58:48,016 --> 00:58:50,416
At the end of the day, you can mix and match everything.

776
00:58:50,576 --> 00:58:55,300
You can either just use flat-based routes or you can just use directory routes.

777
00:58:55,616 --> 00:59:00,496
It does not really matter, but I guess it makes the most sense to just mix and match them.

778
00:59:00,656 --> 00:59:09,252
In my humble opinion, 80% or yeah, 80% of your routes should be directory routes, and maybe 20% should be flat-based routes.

779
00:59:09,616 --> 00:59:12,096
That's my humble opinion, my recommendation.

780
00:59:12,302 --> 00:59:15,136
But again, if you disagree with me, that's totally fine.

781
00:59:15,342 --> 00:59:18,256
You can just use flat routes, it will do the same thing, right?

782
00:59:18,336 --> 00:59:21,348
I mean, it's not that this is not performant or something like that.

783
00:59:21,536 --> 00:59:22,496
It's the same thing.

784
00:59:22,656 --> 00:59:30,336
It's just a different way to create a route, but the end result is the same, the performance is the same, literally everything is the same.

785
00:59:30,606 --> 00:59:34,896
But now you might also ask me, hey Jan, but how can I localize layouts?

786
00:59:35,056 --> 00:59:36,004
What do I mean by that?

787
00:59:36,270 --> 00:59:41,136
So we have this root layout, and this root layout wraps all of our children.

788
00:59:41,216 --> 00:59:42,692
This means all of our routes.

789
00:59:42,990 --> 00:59:46,816
And this also means that this header is literally rendered everywhere.

790
00:59:47,086 --> 00:59:52,851
But what is if you want to have a navbar or a header that is only available on posts route.

791
00:59:53,056 --> 00:59:56,896
So slash posts slash posts123 and so on.

792
00:59:57,056 --> 00:59:59,460
Well, you can create a local layout.

793
00:59:59,790 --> 01:00:09,856
So in the posts folder, I will create another file, and this won't be called index.tsx or anything like that, but rather route.tsx.

794
01:00:10,062 --> 01:00:11,616
And this will create a layout.

795
01:00:11,776 --> 01:00:15,856
And then for the content, instead of saying hello posts, I will say posts.

796
01:00:16,302 --> 01:00:18,752
So uppercase posts nav bar.

797
01:00:19,086 --> 01:00:24,592
So if I go back, you will see right here hello from the header component and then posts navbar.

798
01:00:24,846 --> 01:00:26,564
But wait, where is our content?

799
01:00:26,766 --> 01:00:35,472
Because if I, for example, open the post ID route, right here I render the post ID, the dynamic param, but I don't see anything right here.

800
01:00:35,694 --> 01:00:36,672
What's the problem?

801
01:00:36,846 --> 01:00:38,832
Well, you want to render your children.

802
01:00:38,912 --> 01:00:40,672
Because what did we do in our root?

803
01:00:40,832 --> 01:00:44,992
If I open the root file, we actually rendered our children.

804
01:00:45,232 --> 01:00:49,284
This means we need to literally do the same thing in our route file.

805
01:00:49,552 --> 01:00:50,592
Well, not quite.

806
01:00:50,798 --> 01:00:55,620
You don't want to get your children, but rather you want to render an outlet component.

807
01:00:55,886 --> 01:01:01,392
So if you go to the documentation and search for outlets, then you will find this section.

808
01:01:01,614 --> 01:01:08,644
And inside of here they say nested routing means that routes can be nested within other routes, including the way they render.

809
01:01:08,846 --> 01:01:12,676
So how do we tell our routes where to render this nested content?

810
01:01:12,912 --> 01:01:16,036
Well, you do that by using the outlet component.

811
01:01:16,334 --> 01:01:21,252
So the outlet component is used to render the next potential matching child route.

812
01:01:21,582 --> 01:01:28,571
Outlet, so the component, doesn't take any props and can be rendered anywhere within a routes component tree.

813
01:01:28,912 --> 01:01:33,312
If there is no matching child route, outlet will render null.

814
01:01:33,518 --> 01:01:34,592
So let me show it to you.

815
01:01:34,752 --> 01:01:43,472
Let's go back and then underneath this posts nav bar, but still in the div, I will get the outlet component from Tanstack React Router.

816
01:01:43,632 --> 01:01:44,752
This is self-closing.

817
01:01:44,912 --> 01:01:51,552
And if I now go back, you will see right here, this is the root header, then this is our nav bar, and we see the content.

818
01:01:51,726 --> 01:01:58,832
So if I update the post ID to post underscore ID underscore one two three, then this updates as you would expect.

819
01:01:59,072 --> 01:02:02,980
So essentially what we just did is we created a layout route.

820
01:02:03,214 --> 01:02:07,632
This route.tsx wraps all of the children in the posts folder.

821
01:02:07,792 --> 01:02:14,276
So if you have a posts.post ID route, as we have, then this route is applicable, it renders.

822
01:02:14,592 --> 01:02:18,788
If you have a posts slash edit route, then this route renders.

823
01:02:19,072 --> 01:02:26,912
But if you have, for example, a slash hello route, then this route is not applicable because it is not in the route tree anymore.

824
01:02:27,086 --> 01:02:28,672
It's not nested in there anymore.

825
01:02:28,910 --> 01:02:35,940
So this means if I now, for example, go back again and navigate to slash about, I won't see this navbar anymore.

826
01:02:36,112 --> 01:02:38,992
I just see the route layout and then hello about.

827
01:02:39,406 --> 01:02:45,611
But if I again go back to slash posts and then slash the dynamic param, I again see this navbar.

828
01:02:45,792 --> 01:02:50,992
If you want to visit a route outside of the tree, you won't see this route layout anymore.

829
01:02:51,152 --> 01:02:52,912
That's very important to remember.

830
01:02:53,072 --> 01:02:56,164
And whenever you create one, you want to render the outlet.

831
01:02:56,352 --> 01:02:58,432
The outlet is very similar to children.

832
01:02:58,512 --> 01:03:00,112
It's pretty much the exact same thing.

833
01:03:00,272 --> 01:03:10,308
In the root, you use children to render all of your children, but in every route layout, instead of destructuring children or something like that, you want to use the outlet component.

834
01:03:10,432 --> 01:03:14,672
And as they say here, render the next child match in the route tree.

835
01:03:14,992 --> 01:03:18,112
And you can also, for example, render above your content.

836
01:03:18,286 --> 01:03:28,352
This means if I go back, I will now first of all see my root navbar, then my content, and only then the nav bar, because the outlet is rendered above my content.

837
01:03:28,526 --> 01:03:39,152
If I again rendered underneath my posts navbar, I first of all see my header, my main header, then the navbar, the route layout, and only then I see the main content.

838
01:03:39,312 --> 01:03:41,152
So this is how everything works.

839
01:03:41,358 --> 01:03:48,352
And if I now navigate to slash posts slash my gibberish ID slash edit, I will still see the nav bar.

840
01:03:48,558 --> 01:03:52,900
So hello from the header component, posts nav bar, and then I see the edit route.

841
01:03:52,992 --> 01:03:56,832
So this is very powerful and a very important concept to understand.

842
01:03:57,184 --> 01:04:01,604
Because in most cases, what I, for example, do is create a dashboard route.

843
01:04:01,744 --> 01:04:06,944
And this dashboard has a specific layout with a sidebar and navbar and stuff like that.

844
01:04:07,118 --> 01:04:17,904
And what I do then is create a dashboard folder with an index.tsx file to create an index route and then a route file to create the layout with the navbar and sidebar.

845
01:04:18,144 --> 01:04:21,184
And this means it will always be persisted whenever you navigate.

846
01:04:21,264 --> 01:04:29,764
So when I for example navigate to slash dashboard slash import, the sidebar and navbar won't re-render because they are like the root layout.

847
01:04:29,904 --> 01:04:31,460
They just stay persisted.

848
01:04:31,630 --> 01:04:32,864
And that's very powerful.

849
01:04:33,038 --> 01:04:38,224
If you, for example, already used next.js in the past, then this will be very similar.

850
01:04:38,414 --> 01:04:44,304
Because in Next.js, what you do is create a folder, then a page.tsx to define the route.

851
01:04:44,430 --> 01:04:49,104
And if you want to create a layout, you create a layout.tsx file.

852
01:04:49,264 --> 01:04:59,012
But in this case, you create a folder, as with Next.js, then you create an index file to define the route, and a route file to create the layout.

853
01:04:59,184 --> 01:05:02,784
And instead of passing children, you use the outlet component.

854
01:05:02,958 --> 01:05:05,504
But the outlet component is literally the same thing.

855
01:05:05,744 --> 01:05:09,344
And you can create as many route layouts as you want.

856
01:05:09,504 --> 01:05:14,980
You can create one for the posts route, then one for the post ID route, then one for the edit route.

857
01:05:15,264 --> 01:05:18,304
Like you can create a route layout for each route.

858
01:05:18,544 --> 01:05:19,876
It does not make sense, right?

859
01:05:20,064 --> 01:05:25,584
I don't think that you need a layout for every route, but you have the ability to create one.

