Because whenever you map over data in React, you want to always give the child a unique identifier, a key.

2307
02:51:25,838 --> 02:51:35,072
So this keyword we will give the sidebar menu item a key, and this will be equal to item dot name because the name is a unique value.

2308
02:51:35,310 --> 02:51:42,032
What you can also do is for example get the index, and then instead of passing the name, we can pass the index.

2309
02:51:42,126 --> 02:51:48,740
if you people might say that you should not use the index as a key, and that's correct, that's a best practice.

2310
02:51:48,942 --> 02:51:53,156
But in this case, it does not matter since the key or the items are static.

2311
02:51:53,392 --> 02:51:58,592
If you have items that can be reordered, then you don't want to use the index for the key.

2312
02:51:58,752 --> 02:52:04,192
But when you have static items that don't change in order, you can just use the index.

2313
02:52:04,352 --> 02:52:05,072
It does not matter.

2314
02:52:05,232 --> 02:52:09,712
This is completely fine to do so, and nobody will complain about this code.

2315
02:52:09,872 --> 02:52:18,832
But if this would be dynamic, if you could change the items in order, or if the user could just reorder them using the UI right here, then you would not want to do that.

2316
02:52:18,992 --> 02:52:24,752
You wouldn't want to use the index, but rather some sort of unique key, like for example an ID.

2317
02:52:24,912 --> 02:52:27,952
But since this is static, we will leave it as is.

