5362
06:44:35,168 --> 06:44:38,608
In other words, we want to store the state in the URL.

5363
06:44:38,798 --> 06:44:51,140
We want to add a question mark and then say the input is equal to whatever the user added, and then we can add this AND symbol and say the filter is equal to, I don't know, pending or something like that.

5364
06:44:51,374 --> 06:45:01,968
And this means if the user then does a hard refresh, the URL does not change, and we can then also wire up our input to the URL so that the state in the UI is also not lost.

5365
06:45:02,128 --> 06:45:13,008
And thankfully, Tanstack Start has first class support for search params, and they are actually even typesave, something that you don't really get with other frameworks.

5366
06:45:13,326 --> 06:45:20,608
So if you go to the Tanstack Router documentation and search for search params, then you will find this documentation page.

5367
06:45:20,848 --> 06:45:36,528
And inside of here they say similar to how Tanstack Query made handling server-side state in your React and Solid Applications Breeze, TansStack Router aims to unlock the power of URL search params in your applications.

5368
06:45:36,848 --> 06:45:47,008
And if you scroll a bit to the bottom, they right here also explain why you should use Tanstack Routers implementation instead of using URL Search Params, which is a native web API.

5369
06:45:47,168 --> 06:45:57,728
I would highly recommend you to go through this section just to understand the differences and why this feature is such a game changer, but to save some time I will skip over this.

5370
06:45:57,968 --> 06:46:05,168
If you scroll down a bit further, then you will find this section called Search Params, the OG State Manager.

5371
06:46:05,486 --> 06:46:14,688
And here they say you've probably seen search params like Question Mark page 3 or question Mark FilterName equals to Tana in the URL.

5372
06:46:14,848 --> 06:46:20,448
There is no question that this is truly a form of global state living inside of the URL.

5373
06:46:20,688 --> 06:46:32,768
It's valuable to store specific pieces of the state in the URL because users should be able to command click to open a link in a new tab and reliably see the state they expected.

5374
06:46:33,038 --> 06:46:42,020
Bookmark and share links from your application with others with assurance that they will see exactly the state as when the link was copied.

5375
06:46:42,288 --> 06:46:48,260
And refresh your app or navigate back and forth between pages without losing their state.

5376
06:46:48,494 --> 06:46:54,980
And that's why you will also see a lot of you could say like these big applications always using URL state.

5377
06:46:55,088 --> 06:46:57,968
Like for example, I think you all know what AutoTrader is.

5378
06:46:58,128 --> 06:47:02,608
AutoTrader is there to find cars, new cars, used cars and so on.

5379
06:47:02,798 --> 06:47:06,180
And they, for example, also use URL search params.

5380
06:47:06,542 --> 06:47:11,268
If you look at the URL, then you will see right here that this URL has search params.

5381
06:47:11,568 --> 06:47:19,488
And if I now say, hey, for the price, I want to say I'm rich, only find me cars that are worth more than 60,000 pounds.

5382
06:47:19,568 --> 06:47:22,368
And if I search for this, then the URL will update.

5383
06:47:22,448 --> 06:47:25,380
And that's why URL search params are so powerful.

5384
06:47:25,582 --> 06:47:34,528
Because if I copy the URL and then open it in a new incognito tab, this tab will get the same state with the same filtering, which is very powerful.

5385
06:47:34,862 --> 06:47:48,048
So if we go back to the documentation, then they will say right here developers should be able to easily add, remove, or modify state in the URL with the same great developer experience as other state managers.

5386
06:47:48,512 --> 06:47:56,516
Because a lot of people skip over URL search params and with that URL state because it's often complicated and not type safe.

5387
06:47:56,782 --> 06:48:03,460
But with TanStack Router, everything is type safe, and URL search params are also easy to implement.

5388
06:48:03,758 --> 06:48:19,632
And here they also say easily validate search params coming from the URL in a format and type that is safe for other applications to consume, and finally read and write your search params without having to worry about the underlying serialization format.

5389
06:48:19,872 --> 06:48:21,392
How can we achieve all of this?

5390
06:48:21,518 --> 06:48:26,468
Or in other words, how does Tanstack Router achieve all of these benefits easily?

5391
06:48:26,752 --> 06:48:29,472
Well, by using JSON first search params.

5392
06:48:29,952 --> 06:48:41,632
To achieve the above, the first step built into Tanstack Router is a powerful search param parser that automatically converts the search string of your URL to structure JSON.

5393
06:48:41,872 --> 06:48:50,596
This means that you can store any JSON serializable data structure in your search params and it will be passed and serialized as JSON.

5394
06:48:51,022 --> 06:49:00,912
This is a huge improvement over URL search params, which is a web API which has limited support for array-like structures and nested data.

5395
06:49:01,072 --> 06:49:02,592
I mean look at this example.

5396
06:49:02,798 --> 06:49:17,312
Right here, with just the search property, you can define search params, a page index, right here, in array, something that you are not able to do with the native search params or URL search params API, sort by descending.

5397
06:49:17,472 --> 06:49:18,912
Do you see how easy this is?

5398
06:49:19,086 --> 06:49:21,392
You can add an array, a number, Boolean.

5399
06:49:21,552 --> 06:49:23,312
This is literally groundbreaking.

5400
06:49:23,470 --> 06:49:26,596
And if you look at the URL, then this is nicely added.

5401
06:49:26,798 --> 06:49:34,032
And when the URL is passed, the search params will be accurately converted back into the following JSON structure.

5402
06:49:34,254 --> 06:49:41,668
So yes, the URL looks a bit, you could say, ugly, but once you then convert it back, everything is as you would expect.

5403
06:49:41,966 --> 06:49:47,712
So this is what you define in your code, and this is what you get back, and this is what gets rendered in the URL.

5404
06:49:47,950 --> 06:49:51,152
So if you notice, there are a few things going on here.

5405
06:49:51,342 --> 06:49:57,312
The first level of the search params is flat and string based, just like URL search params.

5406
06:49:57,614 --> 06:50:04,832
This means page index equals to 3, include categories to some gibberish, and then sort by equals to price.

5407
06:50:04,912 --> 06:50:06,592
So this is always like a string.

5408
06:50:06,766 --> 06:50:13,952
And then first level values that are not strings are accurately preserved as actual numbers and booleans.

5409
06:50:14,352 --> 06:50:19,712
Nested data structures are automatically converted to URL-safe JSON strings.

5410
06:50:19,982 --> 06:50:23,712
And now you might say, okay Jan, this actually looks kinda good.

5411
06:50:23,950 --> 06:50:26,352
But how can we validate the search params?

5412
06:50:26,592 --> 06:50:30,992
Well, here they have a section on validating and typing search params.

5413
06:50:31,312 --> 06:50:41,472
Despite TanStack Router being able to pass search params into reliable JSON, they ultimately still come from a user-facing raw text input.

5414
06:50:41,774 --> 06:50:53,124
Similar to other serialization boundaries, this means that before you consume search params, they should be validated into a format that your application can trust and rely on.

5415
06:50:53,358 --> 06:50:57,312
Because here's the thing, in theory, I can add anything in the URL.

5416
06:50:57,472 --> 06:51:02,192
I could say, for example, I don't know, hello equals to Jan or something like that.

5417
06:51:02,432 --> 06:51:05,284
This is something that your application might not expect.

5418
06:51:05,518 --> 06:51:07,940
And therefore you want to validate search params.

5419
06:51:08,206 --> 06:51:16,272
Whatever you get from the client side or from something user-facing has to always be validated because you never know what the user does.

5420
06:51:16,462 --> 06:51:19,552
The user can add some gibberish and then you have to fix it.

5421
06:51:19,694 --> 06:51:25,392
Or in other words, you just want to validate the input because you want to make sure that you get that what you expect.

5422
06:51:25,582 --> 06:51:31,952
And that's why Tan Stack Router provides convenient APIs for validating and typing search params.

5423
06:51:32,272 --> 06:51:36,432
