07:19:32,592 --> 07:19:38,468
If I switch it back into processing, then I won't get any data, and we will render the empty state.

5812
07:19:38,832 --> 07:19:40,352
Nice, we are finished.

5813
07:19:40,558 --> 07:19:47,952
Let's again move this into all statuses or switch it into all statuses, and if I say 11, I will only get one result.

5814
07:19:48,352 --> 07:19:49,712
This works so well.

5815
07:19:49,998 --> 07:19:53,312
And did you see how easy it was to implement everything?

5816
07:19:53,552 --> 07:19:58,980
We literally just implemented fully type safe search params in a matter of minutes.

5817
07:19:59,246 --> 07:20:05,552
And the important thing is that these search params act as our global state management solution.

5818
07:20:05,742 --> 07:20:09,392
We did not install any third-party library or anything like that.

5819
07:20:09,552 --> 07:20:12,512
Also, there was no complex configuration needed.

5820
07:20:12,686 --> 07:20:26,752
All we did is literally just create a sort schema where we defined our search params, or in other words, what we want to get, in this case Q and status, and then we connected the validate search property to our schema.

5821
07:20:26,862 --> 07:20:27,392
And that's it.

5822
07:20:27,598 --> 07:20:30,512
With that, we have fully type safe search params.

5823
07:20:30,752 --> 07:20:34,112
And also we have JSON first search params.

5824
07:20:34,432 --> 07:20:37,872
As you all know, I'm a big NextJS developer.

5825
07:20:38,032 --> 07:20:41,028
And with NextJS, you don't get these features.

5826
07:20:41,294 --> 07:20:48,512
With Next.js, you just get access to the URL search params web API, and then good luck.

5827
07:20:48,718 --> 07:21:01,060
This means yes, you can still create the same experience, you can still read search params and then also update them, but you don't get any type safety, and also you don't get JSON first search params.

5828
07:21:01,422 --> 07:21:07,748
So trust me when I say this, this is a game changer, and I think most people don't really appreciate this.

5829
07:21:07,982 --> 07:21:13,472
But this is one of my most favorite features, and a feature that literally changes everything.

5830
07:21:13,742 --> 07:21:21,712
You don't need to install any third-party library, everything is already handled natively, and this is the perfect state manager.

5831
07:21:21,934 --> 07:21:25,792
And with that out of the way, let's continue with our application.

5832
07:21:26,032 --> 07:21:30,432
And here's the thing if I do a hard refresh, everything is quite quick, right?

5833
07:21:30,638 --> 07:21:36,192
I mean it takes a few milliseconds to get our data, and with that you also fully render the page.

5834
07:21:36,398 --> 07:21:38,912
But how do we get this data right now?

5835
07:21:39,182 --> 07:21:43,872
Well, if we go back to the index file, then you will see that we have a loader.

5836
07:21:44,046 --> 07:21:47,872
And this loader calls the getItems server function.

5837
07:21:48,142 --> 07:21:52,592
Looking at the getItems server function, we use Prisma to get data.

5838
07:21:52,752 --> 07:21:57,700
And this whole you could say Prisma call maybe takes a few milliseconds.

5839
07:21:57,966 --> 07:21:59,792
So everything is quite quick.

5840
07:22:00,014 --> 07:22:02,112
But let's imagine that we would fetch.

5841
07:22:02,624 --> 07:22:04,064
1 trillion records.

5842
07:22:04,224 --> 07:22:10,624
Okay, no, I'm joking, but let's imagine that we would try to fetch, let's say, 10,000 saved items.

5843
07:22:10,864 --> 07:22:14,384
Do you think that this would still take only a few milliseconds?

5844
07:22:14,544 --> 07:22:21,188
No, this would take a few seconds, maybe 10 seconds, 15 seconds, or 20 seconds.

5845
07:22:21,486 --> 07:22:24,804
To show it to you, I will create a fake delay right here.

5846
07:22:25,184 --> 07:22:26,304
5 seconds.

5847
07:22:26,544 --> 07:22:34,304
If I now go back, copy the URL and open it in the new tab, you will see that it will take 5 seconds to get our data.

5848
07:22:34,510 --> 07:22:37,764
So I will click on enter in 3, 2, 1, boom.

5849
07:22:37,984 --> 07:22:39,424
And do we see any data?

5850
07:22:39,584 --> 07:22:44,004
No. 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, and boom.

5851
07:22:44,224 --> 07:22:45,584
I just got my data.

5852
07:22:45,744 --> 07:22:47,264
And this is a huge problem.

5853
07:22:47,504 --> 07:22:54,564
We have a huge render blocker, or in other words, we have a huge blocking event that prevents our site from being rendered.

5854
07:22:54,784 --> 07:22:55,684
And that's not good.

5855
07:22:55,918 --> 07:22:59,904
Because looking at this page, what is currently blocking everything?

5856
07:23:00,110 --> 07:23:00,932
Is it the sidebar?

5857
07:23:01,264 --> 07:23:01,504
No.

5858
07:23:01,664 --> 07:23:03,812
The sidebar, or what do we do in the sidebar?

5859
07:23:04,144 --> 07:23:05,744
We fetch the user session.

5860
07:23:05,824 --> 07:23:08,164
But this only takes a few milliseconds.

5861
07:23:08,494 --> 07:23:10,704
Does this nav bar fetch any data?

5862
07:23:10,864 --> 07:23:11,104
No.

5863
07:23:11,264 --> 07:23:14,464
Does this data or does this section fetch any data?

5864
07:23:14,624 --> 07:23:14,864
No.

5865
07:23:15,024 --> 07:23:17,344
Does this input fetch any data?

5866
07:23:17,504 --> 07:23:17,824
No.

5867
07:23:17,984 --> 07:23:25,028
The only thing that currently blocks the render is literally this little section that fetches our saved items.

5868
07:23:25,358 --> 07:23:27,424
Let me show it to you one more time.

5869
07:23:27,598 --> 07:23:30,852
I will copy the URL and open it in a new tab.

5870
07:23:31,024 --> 07:23:33,764
One, two, three, boom, and we have to wait.

5871
07:23:33,984 --> 07:23:39,504
One second, two seconds, three seconds, four seconds, and only now I get my data.

5872
07:23:39,662 --> 07:23:40,944
And this is a huge problem.

5873
07:23:41,134 --> 07:23:44,144
And now you might say, okay, Jen, what's the solution?

5874
07:23:44,304 --> 07:23:47,664
How can we, I guess, suspend the data loading?

5875
07:23:47,904 --> 07:23:48,624
Good idea.

5876
07:23:48,878 --> 07:23:50,224
So here's what I want to do.

5877
07:23:50,414 --> 07:23:52,464
I want to render the layout instantly.

5878
07:23:52,624 --> 07:23:55,984
What I mean by that is this section right here, let me make it red.

5879
07:23:56,224 --> 07:23:58,944
This section does not fetch any heavy data.

5880
07:23:59,104 --> 07:24:01,028
It can be rendered instantly.

5881
07:24:01,264 --> 07:24:04,464
Only this section takes 5 seconds to load.

5882
07:24:04,750 --> 07:24:10,544
So what is if we could render this here instantly, the top section, and then suspend this data.

5883
07:24:10,704 --> 07:24:20,544
We could, for example, render some sort of loading indicator, telling the user, hey, we are already finished with the layout, here's the layout, but this data takes a bit to render.

5884
07:24:20,718 --> 07:24:23,424
Please wait a bit, it will be there in a second.

5885
07:24:23,694 --> 07:24:27,584
And you implement or this concept is called streaming.

5886
07:24:27,758 --> 07:24:33,424
Sadly, the Tanstack Start documentation does not really have a good section on streaming.

5887
07:24:33,584 --> 07:24:37,024
Therefore, let's go to the next JS documentation.

5888
07:24:37,184 --> 07:24:48,420
And inside of here they say streaming allows the server to send parts of a dynamic route to the client as soon as they are ready rather than waiting for the entire route to be rendered.

5889
07:24:48,654 --> 07:24:53,604
This means users see something sooner, even if parts of the page are still loading.

5890
07:24:53,904 --> 07:24:59,984
Essentially, what we can do is send the layout instantly to the client because there's nothing to wait for.

5891
07:25:00,206 --> 07:25:03,716
Yes, we fetch the user session, but this is almost instant.

5892
07:25:03,918 --> 07:25:08,036
The only thing that takes a bit to fully render is this content.

5893
07:25:08,206 --> 07:25:12,464
And what we can do is suspend the content and show a loading indicator.

5894
07:25:12,654 --> 07:25:19,024
And once the data has finished fetching, we can swap out the loading indicator for the real data.

5895
07:25:19,278 --> 07:25:23,584
So here they have this beautiful image that explains everything quite well.

5896
07:25:23,824 --> 07:25:26,144
This here is our layout, for example.

5897
07:25:26,318 --> 07:25:28,304
And this layout is instantly there.

5898
07:25:28,384 --> 07:25:30,084
It does not need to be suspended.

5899
07:25:30,384 --> 07:25:34,864
The only thing that takes quite a bit of time to be fully fetched is this data.

5900
07:25:35,118 --> 07:25:42,276
So what we can do is show a loading state, as they say here partial content, this is the layout, with loading state.

5901
07:25:42,384 --> 07:25:43,504
This is the loading state.

5902
07:25:43,886 --> 07:25:47,056
And then here they say suspended content streaming in.

5903
07:25:47,216 --> 07:25:49,316
This content is currently being loaded.

5904
07:25:49,536 --> 07:25:52,804
While it's being loaded, we will show a loading state.

5905
07:25:52,942 --> 07:26:00,996
And once the data has finished loading, for example, after 5 seconds, we will swap out the loading state for the real content.

5906
07:26:01,216 --> 07:26:05,764
This is what you call streaming, or in other words, suspended data loading.

5907
07:26:05,936 --> 07:26:21,616
And the interesting thing is after now going through the Tan Stack Router documentation a bit, I also found a section from them also where they talk about this suspended data loading, but they don't call it suspended data loading or streaming or something like that.

5908
07:26:21,856 --> 07:26:24,256
They call it deferred data loading.

5909
07:26:24,416 --> 07:26:27,364
It's essentially the same thing, so I guess who cares?

5910
07:26:27,662 --> 07:26:29,216
Let's quickly see what they say.

5911
07:26:29,550 --> 07:26:39,620
Tanstack Router is designed, and with that also Tanstack Start, is designed to run loaders in parallel and wait for all of them to resolve before rendering the next route.

5912
07:26:39,856 --> 07:26:49,284
This is great most of the time, but occasionally you may want to show the user something sooner while the rest of the data loads in the background.

5913
07:26:49,550 --> 07:27:02,244
Deferred data loading is a pattern that allows the router to render the next location's critical data markup while slower non-critical route data is resolved in the background.

5914
07:27:02,496 --> 07:27:07,056
This process works both on the client and server via streaming.

5915
07:27:07,216 --> 07:27:15,456
Okay, they also mention streaming inside of here, and it's a great way to improve the perceived performance of your application.

5916
07:27:15,776 --> 07:27:16,896
That sounds great.

5917
07:27:17,056 --> 07:27:18,736
And how can we now get started?

5918
07:27:18,896 --> 07:27:21,696
Well, looking at the index file, what do we do?

5919
07:27:21,902 --> 07:27:23,936
We call the getItems function.

5920
07:27:24,110 --> 07:27:25,776
What is the getItems function?

5921
07:27:26,126 --> 07:27:31,204
Oh you might say, hey Jan, don't bore me, this is a server function, we already know that.

5922
07:27:31,536 --> 07:27:32,260
That's correct.

5923
07:27:32,656 --> 07:27:34,576
But what is the server function?

5924
07:27:34,736 --> 07:27:37,456
It's a promise, it's an asynchronous function.

5925
07:27:37,742 --> 07:27:38,976
What do we do right here?

5926
07:27:39,216 --> 07:27:42,724
We await the promise and with that then get the data.

5927
07:27:42,976 --> 07:27:46,336
But with streaming, you don't want to await the promise.

5928
07:27:46,496 --> 07:27:47,696
That's the important thing.

5929
07:27:47,936 --> 07:27:51,936
In this case, what we want to do is return a promise pretty much.

5930
07:27:52,256 --> 07:27:54,416
What I want you to do is update the code.

5931
07:27:54,606 --> 07:27:56,676
Let's delete this getItemsfn.

5932
07:27:56,816 --> 07:28:08,708
And instead of returning getItemsfn, or in other words, awaiting the promise, we will actually create this items promise, and this will be equal to getItemsfn and I will invoke it.

5933
07:28:08,910 --> 07:28:22,468
This code might look a bit weird right now, but essentially what we are doing right here is that we are simply returning an unawaited promise from the loader, and then this will be automatically deferred or in other words streamed.

5934
07:28:22,736 --> 07:28:25,056
Also, in the past you did this a bit differently.

5935
07:28:25,216 --> 07:28:30,656
In the past you used this defer function, and the defer function did the exact same thing.

5936
07:28:30,816 --> 07:28:35,812
What I mean by that is using the defer function you could return an unawaited promise.

5937
07:28:36,176 --> 07:28:44,016
But with newer versions of Tanstack Router and with that Tanstack Start, you can now just return an unawaited promise and that's it.

5938
07:28:44,176 --> 07:28:45,776
Then you are already finished.

5939
07:28:46,016 --> 07:28:52,336
But currently, if you scroll a bit to the bottom, then you will see that we get an error in our route component.

5940
07:28:52,496 --> 07:29:00,016
And to be specific, we get an error for the items list, and that's because data does not match data or something like that.

5941
07:29:00,256 --> 07:29:03,376
What we need to do is update our destructuring.

5942
07:29:03,536 --> 07:29:06,756
Currently, we get data from the use loader data hook.

5943
07:29:06,896 --> 07:29:11,012
But instead of getting data, we want to get the items promise.

5944
07:29:11,310 --> 07:29:17,376
I will destructure it, let's scroll to the bottom, and data will be equal to items promise.

5945
07:29:17,776 --> 07:29:19,616
But we instantly get an error.

5946
07:29:19,776 --> 07:29:20,576
What's the problem?

5947
07:29:20,846 --> 07:29:25,828
Well, if we scroll to the top, what does our items list component expect?

5948
07:29:26,096 --> 07:29:27,616
It expects, where is it?

5949
07:29:27,696 --> 07:29:29,696
Let me scroll to the top a bit further.

5950
07:29:29,936 --> 07:29:31,536
It's it expects data.

5951
07:29:31,696 --> 07:29:33,124
Okay, we already know that.

5952
07:29:33,296 --> 07:29:34,724
But what type is data?

5953
07:29:35,329 --> 07:29:38,148
Awaited return type getItemsfn.

5954
07:29:38,496 --> 07:29:40,900
This is wrong, or this is, I guess, a mistake.

5955
07:29:41,136 --> 07:29:42,976
No, it's not a mistake, but this is incorrect.

5956
07:29:43,424 --> 07:29:45,104
This is not awaited anymore.

5957
07:29:45,344 --> 07:29:50,020
Yes, we awaited the data before, but now this is an unawaited promise.

5958
07:29:50,304 --> 07:29:56,624
Therefore, we need to delete this awaited and just say return type type of getItemsfn.

5959
07:29:56,944 --> 07:30:03,460
And if I now again scroll to the bottom, then you will see that this error will go away because this matches the type.

5960
07:30:03,694 --> 07:30:09,060
Now the items list component throws an error, and that's because the data is unawaited.

5961
07:30:09,264 --> 07:30:13,104
If I hover over filter, it says, hey, did you forget to use await?

5962
07:30:13,344 --> 07:30:15,364
Did you forget to await the data?

5963
07:30:15,598 --> 07:30:19,424
Well, not really, but also kinda, because the data is not awaited.

5964
07:30:19,630 --> 07:30:21,860
It's still an unawaited promise.

5965
07:30:22,094 --> 07:30:33,104
What we need to do inside of here is go above the constant filtered items, and let's say constant items, or yeah, I think let's call it items, and this will be equal to use.

5966
07:30:33,504 --> 07:30:35,184
Use is a React hook.

5967
07:30:35,264 --> 07:30:36,944
Please import it from React.

5968
07:30:37,184 --> 07:30:39,504
And as an argument, I will pass the data.

5969
07:30:39,664 --> 07:30:48,624
Let's now update our whole, you could say, function, or what I mean by that is instead of saying data.filter, let's say items.filter.

5970
07:30:48,704 --> 07:30:51,424
And with that, the error is gone automatically.

5971
07:30:51,584 --> 07:30:54,852
Instead of saying data.length, this will be items.length.

5972
07:30:55,104 --> 07:31:00,740
I will do the same thing right here, items.length, and then also right here, items.length.

5973
07:31:00,944 --> 07:31:02,384
Do we have any other errors?

5974
07:31:02,544 --> 07:31:04,144
No, this all looks good to me.

5975
07:31:04,304 --> 07:31:05,264
And let's test it out.

5976
07:31:05,424 --> 07:31:08,100
And by the way, I will explain the code in a second.

5977
07:31:08,334 --> 07:31:09,344
Don't worry.

5978
07:31:09,518 --> 07:31:12,704
As always, no need to worry, I will explain everything.

5979
07:31:12,910 --> 07:31:18,144
Let's go back, I will do a hard refresh, and as you see here, we don't see anything.

5980
07:31:18,304 --> 07:31:21,104
Wait, this is a bit weird to be quite honest with you.

5981
07:31:21,390 --> 07:31:27,504
We see already this black background color or this dark background, but we did not see any content.

5982
07:31:27,726 --> 07:31:35,024
This was a bit weird because in the previous example, when we actually had our awaited promise, we did not display anything.

5983
07:31:35,104 --> 07:31:40,068
We just saw this default screen because our page was awaiting the promise.

5984
07:31:40,304 --> 07:31:44,624
But since we are now not awaiting the promise anymore, we see the background.

5985
07:31:44,704 --> 07:31:51,172
We don't see anything like we don't render the sidebar or anything like that, but we still see this dark background.

5986
07:31:51,584 --> 07:31:52,864
This is interesting.

5987
07:31:53,102 --> 07:31:54,624
What's exactly the problem?

5988
07:31:54,830 --> 07:32:03,424
Well, currently we literally suspend everything, but we don't want to suspend everything, but instead only the items list component.

5989
07:32:03,598 --> 07:32:08,224
And what we can do is wrap the component in a suspense boundary.

5990
07:32:08,558 --> 07:32:14,116
Suspense is imported from React, and I will paste the content or the component inside of here.

5991
07:32:14,304 --> 07:32:28,292
If I go back and do a hard refresh, we see the content instantly, we see the sidebar instantly and the top section, and only the items, the saved items, are currently being suspended or in other words, deferred.

5992
07:32:28,590 --> 07:32:31,876
And now you might say, Jan, I'm kinda confused.

5993
07:32:32,142 --> 07:32:35,104
What is or what does this suspense boundary do?

5994
07:32:35,214 --> 07:32:37,024
And what does this use hook do?

5995
07:32:37,104 --> 07:32:38,020
What even is it?

5996
07:32:38,224 --> 07:32:49,444
Well, if you go to the React documentation, then you will see that use is a React API that lets you read the value of a resource like a promise or context.

5997
07:32:49,984 --> 07:32:57,584
Essentially, using this use hook, we can consume the promise, show a loading state, and also await the real data.

5998
07:32:57,744 --> 07:32:59,824
And this use hook is relatively new.

5999
07:32:59,904 --> 07:33:07,584
It got introduced, I think, with React 19, and in the past with React 18, you used the await component.

6000
07:33:07,744 --> 07:33:11,664
And the await component was there to resolve the deferred promise.

6001
07:33:11,744 --> 07:33:14,304
And using the use hook, you can do the same thing.

6002
07:33:14,464 --> 07:33:18,064
You can resolve the deferred promise and then render the data.

6003
07:33:18,254 --> 07:33:20,864
And that's also what they mentioned in the documentation.

6004
07:33:21,038 --> 07:33:25,344
In React 19, you can use the use hook instead of await.

6005
07:33:25,424 --> 07:33:30,944
And since we use React 19, it also makes sense to use the newest hooks, if that makes sense.

6006
07:33:31,214 --> 07:33:37,184
So instead of using the old await component, we rather use the native use hook, which does the same thing.

6007
07:33:37,358 --> 07:33:45,764
So to summarize everything one more time, instead of returning or instead of awaiting our data, we now return an unawaited promise.

6008
07:33:46,064 --> 07:33:50,624
This unawaited promise is then passed as a child to the component.

6009
07:33:50,704 --> 07:33:52,864
In this case, to the how is it called?

6010
07:33:53,024 --> 07:33:54,304
Items list component.

6011
07:33:54,464 --> 07:34:02,948
Because as you see here in the route component, we get this unawaited promise, items promise, and then we pass it as a prop to the component.

6012
07:34:03,264 --> 07:34:07,984
The items list component gets this unawaited promise as you see here.

6013
07:34:08,064 --> 07:34:08,944
So where is it?

6014
07:34:09,024 --> 07:34:09,344
Data.

6015
07:34:09,504 --> 07:34:11,204
This is this unawaited promise.

6016
07:34:11,374 --> 07:34:17,284
And instead of using the data, we pretty much wrap it in this use hook or we pass it as an argument.

6017
07:34:17,424 --> 07:34:24,836
And the use hook is able to read this unawaited promise and then to resolve it, which means we can then use the data.

6018
07:34:25,102 --> 07:34:30,436
And now you might also ask me, hey Jan, what is the suspense component and why do we need it?

6019
07:34:30,816 --> 07:34:39,776
Well, if you go to the React documentation, then you will see that suspense lets you display a fallback until its children have finished loading.

6020
07:34:40,016 --> 07:34:48,816
Essentially, using the suspense component, we can tell our application, hey, only this component has unawaited data which will be awaited.

6021
07:34:48,976 --> 07:34:52,576
Please defer the data loading and show a loading indicator.

6022
07:34:52,750 --> 07:34:58,212
Currently we don't show a loading indicator, but using the suspense component, we can do that.

6023
07:34:58,496 --> 07:35:03,044
