08:30:59,438 --> 08:31:00,868
So what did we do in the past?

6764
08:31:01,072 --> 08:31:03,588
We always created server functions.

6765
08:31:03,982 --> 08:31:12,772
Server functions are like API endpoints because they run on the server side, but they can be called from both the client side and server side.

6766
08:31:13,072 --> 08:31:19,392
The only difference is that an API endpoint is specifically designed to be globally available.

6767
08:31:19,598 --> 08:31:26,992
A server function is also a public endpoint, but it hasn't been designed with the idea of being publicly available.

6768
08:31:27,214 --> 08:31:34,432
So you don't want to really use it to, for example, make a request to your endpoint from a mobile application or something like that.

6769
08:31:34,672 --> 08:31:36,592
This is like a server action.

6770
08:31:36,832 --> 08:31:42,084
If you want to have a publicly available endpoint, then create an API endpoint.

6771
08:31:42,318 --> 08:31:48,548
Both do the same thing, both are still publicly available, both have to still be, I guess, authenticated or protected.

6772
08:31:48,752 --> 08:31:57,232
But an API endpoint is designed to be globally available, and a server endpoint is designed to be local but at the same time still public.

6773
08:31:57,358 --> 08:32:00,912
I know this sounds confusing, but in general, remember the following.

6774
08:32:01,072 --> 08:32:04,932
If you want to have a global endpoint, create an API endpoint.

6775
08:32:05,152 --> 08:32:09,232
If you want to have a private endpoint, then use a server function.

6776
08:32:09,422 --> 08:32:13,156
But remember, a server function is still like an API endpoint.

6777
08:32:13,326 --> 08:32:15,872
And what I mean by that is it's still public.

6778
08:32:16,206 --> 08:32:22,180
