7213
09:04:00,878 --> 09:04:06,704
And if you want to learn more about this invalidation, then I would recommend you to go through the documentation.

7214
09:04:06,894 --> 09:04:12,144
Because inside of here they say Tanstack Router comes with short-term caching built in.

7215
09:04:12,334 --> 09:04:26,144
So even though we are not storing any data after a route match is unmounted, there is a high probability that if any mutations are made related to the data stored in the router, the current router matches data could become stale.

7216
09:04:26,304 --> 09:04:35,424
When mutations related to a loader data are made, we can use router.invalidate to force the router to reload all of the current route matches.

7217
09:04:35,664 --> 09:04:47,344
Invalidating all of the current matches happens in the background, so existing data will continue to be served until new data is ready, just as if you were navigating to a new route.

7218
09:04:47,566 --> 09:04:56,224
So when you use this invalidate method, it invalidates the full router, which means it refetches all of the data, and all of that is done in the background.

7219
09:04:56,384 --> 09:05:01,584
Once the data has been refetched, it then reloads everything and renders the new data.

7220
09:05:01,744 --> 09:05:02,864
Isn't that powerful?

7221
09:05:03,104 --> 09:05:13,156
This is very similar to what you have in Next.js, because in Nextjs you also have a cache that you need to invalidate, or in other words, revalidate.

7222
09:05:13,424 --> 09:05:18,464
But here instead of revalidating it, they call it invalidation, which is also fine.

7223
09:05:18,624 --> 09:05:27,652
Essentially, you invalidate all of the queries and then refetch the data in the background, and with that you will get new data and also render it.

