7646
09:35:38,222 --> 09:35:40,260
That's what we do in our items route.

7647
09:35:40,576 --> 09:35:44,336
We suspend the data loading and we show a fallback indicator.

7648
09:35:44,526 --> 09:35:47,364
But now we want to implement streaming for a mutation.

7649
09:35:47,630 --> 09:35:56,804
So essentially, if you go to the documentation and search for streaming data from server functions, and by the way, they updated the documentation, it looks quite nice.

7650
09:35:57,038 --> 09:36:04,036
And here they say streaming data from the server has become very popular thanks to the rise of AI applications.

7651
09:36:04,366 --> 09:36:10,736
Luckily, it's pretty easy with TanStack Start, and what's even better, the streamed data is typed.

7652
09:36:11,118 --> 09:36:18,788
The two most popular ways of streaming data from server functions are using readable streams or async generators.

7653
09:36:19,088 --> 09:36:22,404
You can see how to implement both in these examples.

7654
09:36:22,688 --> 09:36:27,568
But essentially, what we can now do is stream data from our server function.

7655
09:36:27,728 --> 09:36:34,688
And then we can say, hey, URL 1 or 30 has been imported, URL2 or 30 has been imported, and so on.

7656
09:36:34,862 --> 09:36:41,860
We can give the user progress updates, and that's a game-changing feature that a lot of applications don't have.

7657
09:36:42,128 --> 09:36:45,408
But our application will have such a feature.

7658
09:36:45,614 --> 09:36:49,604
And there are two ways to implement streaming data from server functions.

7659
09:36:49,888 --> 09:36:56,288
You can either use typed readable streams or you can use async generators in server functions.

7660
09:36:56,368 --> 09:36:57,568
And that's what we will do.

7661
09:36:57,648 --> 09:37:02,724
Because this is a much cleaner approach, which gets the same result and it's easier to write.

7662
09:37:02,848 --> 09:37:05,444
That's why we will use async generators.

